333 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 198 0 R
/E (Visualizing Knowledge, Framing Evidence: A Diachronic Study of Graphical Markers in Social Science Research Writing)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
334 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 198 0 R
/E (As social science research becomes increasingly data-driven, tables, figures, and statistical visualizations play a more prominent role in scholarly communication. Yet the linguistic devices used to introduce and interpret these visual elements, known as graphical markers, remain underexplored. This keynote presents findings from a diachronic corpus-based study of research articles in Applied Linguistics, Education, and Management across 2005, 2015, and 2025. Drawing on a multidimensional analytical framework, the study traces changing patterns in how writers reference and integrate visual evidence. The results reveal a general increase in graphical markers from 2005 to 2015 across all disciplines, followed by continued growth in Applied Linguistics and Education but a decline in Management. Significant disciplinary differences are found in the use of non-integral and contextual markers, reflecting contrasting rhetorical approaches to presenting evidence. The keynote argues that graphical markers offer an important lens for understanding evolving disciplinary practices, academic argumentation, and the relationship between language and visualized knowledge.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
335 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 198 0 R
/E (Muhammad Afzaal is an Associate Professor at the Institute of Corpus Studies and Applications, Shanghai International Studies University. He earned his PhD from Shanghai Jiao Tong University and has held a research fellowship at The Hong Kong Polytechnic University. His work spans corpus linguistics, discourse studies, and language technology. He has authored books on Belt and Road discourse and technology in applied linguistics, and developed LexiConc, a tool for concordance and lexical-bundle analysis.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
