/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 212 0 R
/T (Research article \(RA\) titles constitute a highly conventionalized micro-genre that performs essential communicative functions in academic discourse. Despite their brevity, titles encode important disciplinary norms, including preferred rhetorical structures, levels of information density, and strategies of reader engagement. Because they are both compact and highly visible, RA titles provide valuable material for investigating how disciplinary communities package and communicate knowledge.)
/E (Research article \(RA\) titles constitute a highly conventionalized micro-genre that performs essential communicative functions in academic discourse. Despite their brevity, titles encode important disciplinary norms, including preferred rhetorical structures, levels of information density, and strategies of reader engagement. Because they are both compact and highly visible, RA titles provide valuable material for investigating how disciplinary communities package and communicate knowledge.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
538 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 212 0 R
/T (This paper examines structural and rhetorical patterns in research article titles across four disciplines representing both soft and hard sciences: Linguistics, Political Science, Genetics, and Condensed Matter Physics. The study is based on a corpus of more than 12,000 titles extracted from leading international journals in each field. The analysis combines quantitative corpus methods with qualitative genre analysis to identify disciplinary conventions in title length, structural complexity, and rhetorical framing.)
/E (This paper examines structural and rhetorical patterns in research article titles across four disciplines representing both soft and hard sciences: Linguistics, Political Science, Genetics, and Condensed Matter Physics. The study is based on a corpus of more than 12,000 titles extracted from leading international journals in each field. The analysis combines quantitative corpus methods with qualitative genre analysis to identify disciplinary conventions in title length, structural complexity, and rhetorical framing.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
539 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 212 0 R
/T (The findings reveal clear cross-disciplinary differences. Titles in the soft sciences \(Linguistics and Political Science\) tend to exhibit greater syntactic complexity and variation, frequently incorporating multi-part constructions, interrogatives, and occasionally metaphorical or allusive elements. In contrast, titles in the hard sciences \(Genetics and Condensed Matter Physics\) are typically more informationally dense, favoring compact nominal structures. These differences reflect broader epistemic orientations: interpretive disciplines appear to allow greater rhetorical flexibility, whereas experimental sciences prioritize precision and terminological specificity.)
/E (The findings reveal clear cross-disciplinary differences. Titles in the soft sciences \(Linguistics and Political Science\) tend to exhibit greater syntactic complexity and variation, frequently incorporating multi-part constructions, interrogatives, and occasionally metaphorical or allusive elements. In contrast, titles in the hard sciences \(Genetics and Condensed Matter Physics\) are typically more informationally dense, favoring compact nominal structures. These differences reflect broader epistemic orientations: interpretive disciplines appear to allow greater rhetorical flexibility, whereas experimental sciences prioritize precision and terminological specificity.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
540 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 212 0 R
/T (Beyond documenting these structural differences, the paper argues that RA titles represent a valuable dataset for genre-based pedagogy. Their brevity and accessibility facilitate pedagogical use in academic writing instruction, particularly in interdisciplinary classrooms. The findings therefore contribute both to research on disciplinary discourse and to the development of corpus-informed approaches to teaching academic writing.)
/E (Beyond documenting these structural differences, the paper argues that RA titles represent a valuable dataset for genre-based pedagogy. Their brevity and accessibility facilitate pedagogical use in academic writing instruction, particularly in interdisciplinary classrooms. The findings therefore contribute both to research on disciplinary discourse and to the development of corpus-informed approaches to teaching academic writing.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
