/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 214 0 R
/T (Purpose: This work-in-progress paper proposes an operational taxonomy of OCR noise for historical print corpora and argues that error modeling itself should be treated as a core methodological step in corpus preparation. Rather than approaching OCR correction primarily as a general cleaning workflow, the study focuses on describing recurrent error types in a way that supports transparent, corpus-sensitive preprocessing and more reliable linguistic analysis.)
/E (Purpose: This work-in-progress paper proposes an operational taxonomy of OCR noise for historical print corpora and argues that error modeling itself should be treated as a core methodological step in corpus preparation. Rather than approaching OCR correction primarily as a general cleaning workflow, the study focuses on describing recurrent error types in a way that supports transparent, corpus-sensitive preprocessing and more reliable linguistic analysis.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
573 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 214 0 R
/T (Methodology: The paper develops a taxonomy of OCR noise for late nineteenth-and early twentieth-century English print materials, organized into functionally distinct classes \(e.g., character confusions, line-break and hyphenation artifacts, layout intrusions, segmentation inconsistencies, and non-lexical token pollution\). For each class, the study defines observable criteria, typical textual signatures, and class-specific correction heuristics. A pilot dataset is manually examined to refine category boundaries and identify ambiguous cases. The taxonomy is then applied to a small corpus sample to generate error-class profiles and targeted preprocessing decisions, followed by corpus-oriented diagnostics comparing untreated and class-targeted outputs.)
/E (Methodology: The paper develops a taxonomy of OCR noise for late nineteenth-and early twentieth-century English print materials, organized into functionally distinct classes \(e.g., character confusions, line-break and hyphenation artifacts, layout intrusions, segmentation inconsistencies, and non-lexical token pollution\). For each class, the study defines observable criteria, typical textual signatures, and class-specific correction heuristics. A pilot dataset is manually examined to refine category boundaries and identify ambiguous cases. The taxonomy is then applied to a small corpus sample to generate error-class profiles and targeted preprocessing decisions, followed by corpus-oriented diagnostics comparing untreated and class-targeted outputs.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
574 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 214 0 R
/T (Summary of findings/results \(preliminary\): Preliminary results suggest that OCR noise is patterned rather than random and that a limited number of recurring classes account for most high-impact distortions across the sample. Class-targeted preprocessing appears to offer a more methodologically stable alternative than undifferentiated cleaning, reducing persistent non-lexical artifacts and improving the interpretability of frequency-based and phraseological outputs.)
/E (Summary of findings/results \(preliminary\): Preliminary results suggest that OCR noise is patterned rather than random and that a limited number of recurring classes account for most high-impact distortions across the sample. Class-targeted preprocessing appears to offer a more methodologically stable alternative than undifferentiated cleaning, reducing persistent non-lexical artifacts and improving the interpretability of frequency-based and phraseological outputs.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
575 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 214 0 R
/T (Conclusion: A taxonomy-based error model offers a principled alternative to ad hoc OCR correction by making preprocessing decisions explicit, revisable, and corpus-sensitive.)
/E (Conclusion: A taxonomy-based error model offers a principled alternative to ad hoc OCR correction by making preprocessing decisions explicit, revisable, and corpus-sensitive.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
