/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 216 0 R
/T (Terminological consistency in multilingual institutional discourse remains a critical challenge, particularly in the context of United Nations \(UN\) documentation, where semantic precision directly affects legal interpretation, policy implementation, and cross-cultural communication. This study explores the principles and practices of terminological alignment in UN documents through the lens of parallel corpus methodology, with special attention to Uzbek-language translations. The research is grounded in a specially compiled parallel corpus consisting of aligned UN texts in English and Uzbek, enabling systematic comparison of lexical, semantic, and pragmatic features across languages.)
/E (Terminological consistency in multilingual institutional discourse remains a critical challenge, particularly in the context of United Nations \(UN\) documentation, where semantic precision directly affects legal interpretation, policy implementation, and cross-cultural communication. This study explores the principles and practices of terminological alignment in UN documents through the lens of parallel corpus methodology, with special attention to Uzbek-language translations. The research is grounded in a specially compiled parallel corpus consisting of aligned UN texts in English and Uzbek, enabling systematic comparison of lexical, semantic, and pragmatic features across languages.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
606 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 216 0 R
/T (The study proposes a multi-level framework for terminological alignment that integrates linguistic, cognitive, and corpus-driven approaches. At the macro level, alignment is achieved through the identification of domain-specific conceptual structures and their standardized representation across languages. At the micro level, the methodology involves sentence-level and term-level alignment using concordance tools, frequency analysis, and context-sensitive mapping of equivalents. Special emphasis is placed on resolving issues of non-equivalence, polysemy, and culturally bound terms through strategies such as borrowing, calquing, descriptive translation, and functional adaptation.)
/E (The study proposes a multi-level framework for terminological alignment that integrates linguistic, cognitive, and corpus-driven approaches. At the macro level, alignment is achieved through the identification of domain-specific conceptual structures and their standardized representation across languages. At the micro level, the methodology involves sentence-level and term-level alignment using concordance tools, frequency analysis, and context-sensitive mapping of equivalents. Special emphasis is placed on resolving issues of non-equivalence, polysemy, and culturally bound terms through strategies such as borrowing, calquing, descriptive translation, and functional adaptation.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
607 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 216 0 R
/T (In the case of Uzbek translations of UN documents, the research identifies several recurring challenges, including inconsistencies in rendering key institutional terms, variation in syntactic structures, and the influence of intermediary languages such as Russian. The study demonstrates that a corpus-based approach allows for the detection of these inconsistencies and supports the development of standardized terminological databases and translation guidelines. Furthermore, the integration of corpus tools with expert validation enhances both the reliability and reproducibility of terminological decisions.)
/E (In the case of Uzbek translations of UN documents, the research identifies several recurring challenges, including inconsistencies in rendering key institutional terms, variation in syntactic structures, and the influence of intermediary languages such as Russian. The study demonstrates that a corpus-based approach allows for the detection of these inconsistencies and supports the development of standardized terminological databases and translation guidelines. Furthermore, the integration of corpus tools with expert validation enhances both the reliability and reproducibility of terminological decisions.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
608 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 216 0 R
/T (The findings highlight the importance of parallel corpora as a methodological foundation for achieving terminological alignment in multilingual governance contexts. The proposed framework contributes to improving the quality, consistency, and interoperability of Uzbek translations of UN documents and offers scalable solutions for other low-resource languages in international institutional settings.)
/E (The findings highlight the importance of parallel corpora as a methodological foundation for achieving terminological alignment in multilingual governance contexts. The proposed framework contributes to improving the quality, consistency, and interoperability of Uzbek translations of UN documents and offers scalable solutions for other low-resource languages in international institutional settings.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
