/Title (In contemporary translation studies, sociolinguistic competence is considered a crucial component of professional translator training, as translators must understand not only linguistic structures but also sociocultural patterns of language use. This study explores the development of sociolinguistic competence among future translators through a gender-oriented pedagogical approach supported by corpus linguistics. In particular, the research utilizes the Corpus of Contemporary American English \(COCA\) as a corpus-based resource for analyzing gender-related language patterns and discourse practices.)
/Dest [37 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2532 0 R
/Prev 2566 0 R
/Next 2588 0 R
>>
endobj
2588 0 obj
<<
/Title (The study aims to design and evaluate a corpus-informed instructional model that integrates gender-sensitive analysis into translator education. The research methodology combines theoretical analysis, corpus-based linguistic investigation, curriculum analysis, classroom observation, surveys, and pedagogical experimentation. Using the COCA corpus, students were engaged in data-driven learning activities that involved identifying gender-marked lexical choices, discourse strategies, and pragmatic features in authentic texts across different registers.)
/Dest [37 0 R /Fit]
