/Title (Research article \(RA\) titles constitute a highly conventionalized micro-genre that performs essential communicative functions in academic discourse. Despite their brevity, titles encode important disciplinary norms, including preferred rhetorical structures, levels of information density, and strategies of reader engagement. Because they are both compact and highly visible, RA titles provide valuable material for investigating how disciplinary communities package and communicate knowledge.)
/Dest [30 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2579 0 R
/Prev 2611 0 R
/Next 2628 0 R
>>
endobj
2628 0 obj
<<
/Title (This paper examines structural and rhetorical patterns in research article titles across four disciplines representing both soft and hard sciences: Linguistics, Political Science, Genetics, and Condensed Matter Physics. The study is based on a corpus of more than 12,000 titles extracted from leading international journals in each field. The analysis combines quantitative corpus methods with qualitative genre analysis to identify disciplinary conventions in title length, structural complexity, and rhetorical framing.)
/Dest [30 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2579 0 R
