2655 0 obj
<<
/Title (Purpose: This work-in-progress paper proposes an operational taxonomy of OCR noise for historical print corpora and argues that error modeling itself should be treated as a core methodological step in corpus preparation. Rather than approaching OCR correction primarily as a general cleaning workflow, the study focuses on describing recurrent error types in a way that supports transparent, corpus-sensitive preprocessing and more reliable linguistic analysis.)
/Dest [32 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2617 0 R
/Prev 2652 0 R
/Next 2656 0 R
>>
endobj
2656 0 obj
<<
/Title (Methodology: The paper develops a taxonomy of OCR noise for late nineteenth-and early twentieth-century English print materials, organized into functionally distinct classes \(e.g., character confusions, line-break and hyphenation artifacts, layout intrusions, segmentation inconsistencies, and non-lexical token pollution\). For each class, the study defines observable criteria, typical textual signatures, and class-specific correction heuristics. A pilot dataset is manually examined to refine category boundaries and identify ambiguous cases. The taxonomy is then applied to a small corpus sample to generate error-class profiles and targeted preprocessing decisions, followed by corpus-oriented diagnostics comparing untreated and class-targeted outputs.)
/Dest [32 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 2617 0 R
/Prev 2655 0 R
/Next 2653 0 R
>>
endobj
